+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by how much they matter, worst first. Line numbers refer to the current file.
+
+Tool results: `selene` and `stylua --check` are clean. `luau-lsp analyze` reports one warning
+(ImplicitReturn in `getOwnerName`, see finding 9). None of the problems below are caught by these
+tools, because the type annotations on remote handlers tell the checker the arguments are
+already the right type.
+
+---
+
+## Critical: exploitable or loses player data
+
+### 1. Remote arguments are never checked at runtime
+**Where:** handlers at lines 84, 105, 127.
+
+**What's wrong:** Annotations like `bt: string, amt: number` are only for the type checker. An
+exploiter can send any value, and several of those values get through:
+
+- **`amt` = NaN** (`0/0`): `amt < 1 or amt > MAX_PURCHASE` is false for NaN, so the check passes.
+  `price` becomes NaN, `coins < NaN` is false, and `SetAsync` is asked to save NaN as the
+  player's coins. That either errors or corrupts the saved value.
+- **`amt` = a fraction** such as `1.5`: passes the range check and gives the player fractional
+  coins and fractional stock.
+- **`amt` or `bt` of the wrong type, or an unknown `bt`**: `PRICES[bt] * amt` throws. This only
+  breaks that one call, but it lets anyone spam errors into the server log.
+- **`pos` with a NaN component**: `PlacementRules.snap` returns NaN. In `PlacementRules.check`,
+  every comparison against NaN is false, so the height check (`center.Y < 0 or > MaxHeight`)
+  and the reach check (`Magnitude > MaxReach`) **both pass**. A NaN X or Z gets past reach,
+  so the player can place a block with no distance limit and possibly at a broken position.
+- **`part` = nil or not an Instance** (line 128): `part.Parent` throws.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type the handler parameters as `unknown` and narrow them before use, so
+the checker makes you validate:
+
+```luau
+buyRemote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then return end
+	if typeof(amount) ~= "number" or amount ~= amount or amount % 1 ~= 0 then return end
+	if amount < 1 or amount > MAX_PURCHASE then return end
+	...
+```
+
+For `pos`, require `typeof(pos) == "Vector3"` and finite components (`x == x and math.abs(x) ~= math.huge`).
+Also consider making `PlacementRules.check` reject non-finite centers itself, so it is safe no
+matter who calls it. For `part`, require `typeof(part) == "Instance" and part:IsA("BasePart")`.
+
+### 2. Coins use DataStore read-modify-write on every purchase (double-spend, throttling, unhandled errors)
+**Where:** `getCoins` / `setCoins` (lines 42–48), used at lines 90–94.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+- **Double-spend race.** `GetAsync` yields. If a client fires `BuyBlocks` twice quickly, both
+  handlers read the same balance, both pass the `coins < price` check, and both write
+  `coins - price`. The player gets two purchases and pays for one. Easy to trigger on purpose.
+- **Throttling.** Every purchase makes two DataStore requests. DataStore budgets are shared by
+  the whole server, so one player spamming the remote can run the budget out and make saves
+  fail for everyone.
+- **No error handling.** `GetAsync` / `SetAsync` throw when the service fails or throttles.
+  Nothing wraps them in `pcall`. If `SetAsync` throws, the purchase silently fails. If it
+  succeeds and something later in the handler throws (see finding 4), the coins are gone and
+  the player gets nothing.
+
+**What to do instead:** Keep each player's balance in memory as the source of truth during a
+session. Load it on `PlayerAdded` inside `pcall` with retries, and don't let the player buy until
+it has loaded. Save on `PlayerRemoving`, in `game:BindToClose`, and every few minutes, using
+`UpdateAsync` inside `pcall`. A purchase then only does an in-memory check-and-subtract, which
+can't yield, so the race goes away. If you'd rather not build this yourself, use a session-locking
+data library (for example ProfileStore).
+
+### 3. Removing someone else's abandoned block credits it to the remover
+**Where:** lines 132–139.
+
+**What's wrong:** The comment says anyone can *clear away* a block whose owner left. But line 139
+then adds that block to the **remover's** stock. Anyone can take every block an absent player
+placed and reuse it for free. And because `getOwnerName` only looks at players who are in the
+server *right now*, this includes a player who has just dropped and is rejoining.
+
+**What to do instead:** Only refund stock when `part:GetAttribute("OwnerId") == player.UserId`.
+For abandoned blocks, destroy them without crediting anyone (or decide on purpose to give them
+to the clearer and write that rule down).
+
+### 4. `stock[plr]` can be nil, and the buy handler takes coins before it finds out
+**Where:** `PlayerAdded` at line 142. Uses at lines 96–97, 106–107, 139.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+- Players who joined before the `PlayerAdded` connection was made never get a `stock` entry.
+  This happens all the time in Studio play-solo. Every handler then fails on `stock[plr][...]`.
+- `PlayerRemoving` sets `stock[plr] = nil`. A buy that is still waiting on `GetAsync` when the
+  player leaves will resume, call `setCoins`, and then throw at line 97. **The coins are taken
+  and no stock is given.** Line 94 subtracts coins before line 96 checks for stock.
+
+**What to do instead:** After connecting `PlayerAdded`, loop over `Players:GetPlayers()` and set
+up any player already in the server. In each handler, get `stock[player]` first and return early
+if it's nil. Update stock before (or at the same moment as) taking coins, so a failure can't
+leave the player charged with nothing to show for it. Moving coins into memory (finding 2)
+removes the yield that causes most of this.
+
+### 5. Stock that players paid for disappears when they leave
+**Where:** `stock` table (line 40), `PlayerRemoving` (line 146).
+
+**What's wrong:** Coins are saved, but the blocks bought with them only exist in memory and are
+wiped on leave. A player who buys 50 blocks and disconnects has lost the coins for good. Their
+placed blocks also stay in the world after they leave, where other players can take them
+(finding 3).
+
+**What to do instead:** Save stock in the same per-player record as coins (finding 2). Then
+decide what happens to placed blocks on leave: clean them up and refund them, keep them saved,
+or leave them for others to clear. Write the choice in the header comment.
+
+---
+
+## High: wrong behavior
+
+### 6. Players can never turn receipts off
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS` gives `true` when
+the attribute is `false`, because `false or true` is `true`. The setting has no effect.
+`AutoAnchor` at lines 118–121 handles this correctly.
+
+**What to do instead:** Use the same nil check as `AutoAnchor`, ideally through a small shared
+helper:
+
+```luau
+local function getSetting(player: Player, name: string, default: boolean): boolean
+	local value = player:GetAttribute(name)
+	if typeof(value) == "boolean" then
+		return value
+	end
+	return default
+end
+```
+
+### 7. Unanchored blocks give clients physics control and break the grid
+**Where:** lines 118–124, `placeBlock` line 71.
+
+**What's wrong:** When `AutoAnchor` is false, the server creates unanchored parts. Roblox hands
+network ownership of unanchored parts to nearby clients, so an exploiter can move or fling them
+anywhere. Even without exploits, the blocks fall or get pushed out of their grid cells, so the
+`Occupied` check and snapping no longer describe the real world.
+
+**What to do instead:** Decide whether unanchored blocks are a real feature. If not, always
+anchor. If so, call `part:SetNetworkOwner(nil)` after parenting to keep the server in control,
+and expect that grid occupancy can drift.
+
+### 8. No rate limits or caps on the remotes
+**Where:** all three handlers. Also `warn` at line 114 and `print` at line 79.
+
+**What's wrong:** A client can fire these remotes as often as it likes. Each failed placement
+calls `warn`, each successful one calls `print`, and each buy makes DataStore calls. That's log
+spam and wasted server work at best, and DataStore exhaustion at worst (finding 2). There is
+also no cap on how many blocks one player can have placed, so repeated buy-and-place can fill
+the server with parts.
+
+**What to do instead:** Add a simple per-player cooldown or token bucket on each remote. Remove
+the `warn` for rejected placements, or send the reason back to that client instead. Put a cap on
+placed blocks per player if part count matters.
+
+---
+
+## Medium: maintainability and type safety
+
+### 9. Ownership is checked by comparing names as strings
+**Where:** `getOwnerName` (lines 50–57) and lines 133–136.
+
+**What's wrong:** The code turns the owner's UserId into a name, turns *that* into a string with
+`tostring`, and compares against the literal `"nil"`. That's hard to read, compares names when
+it should compare IDs, and is the cause of the luau-lsp ImplicitReturn warning (no return on the
+not-found path, no return type). In theory a player whose username is `nil` would have their
+blocks treated as abandoned.
+
+**What to do instead:** Compare IDs directly:
+
+```luau
+local ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+local isOwner = ownerId == player.UserId
+local ownerPresent = typeof(ownerId) == "number" and Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) ~= nil
+if not isOwner and ownerPresent then
+	return
+end
+```
+
+Then `getOwnerName` can be deleted.
+
+### 10. Prices are a separate list from the block types, keyed by any string
+**Where:** `PRICES` (lines 14–20), `placeBlock` cast at line 66.
+
+**What's wrong:** `PRICES` is `{ [string]: number }`, so adding a block type to `BlockTypes` and
+forgetting a price (or making a typo) isn't caught until runtime. `placeBlock` takes
+`bt: string` and casts it to `BlockTypes.BlockType`, which hides the fact that nothing has been
+validated.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type it as `{ [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }`, or put `Price` on each
+entry in `BlockTypes.Styles` so there is one table per block type. Once the remote arguments are
+validated (finding 1), pass `BlockTypes.BlockType` through to `placeBlock` and remove the cast.
+
+### 11. The script isn't in strict mode
+**Where:** top of the file.
+
+**What's wrong:** The shared modules use `--!strict`; this one doesn't. Some of the gaps above,
+such as the missing return type on `getOwnerName`, would be easier to see in strict mode.
+
+**What to do instead:** Add `--!strict` as the first line and fix whatever it reports.
+
+---
+
+## Low: cleanup
+
+### 12. `placeBlock` has a flag that is always the same, and a return value nobody uses
+**Where:** lines 59–82, call at line 124.
+
+`log` is always `true`, so the parameter does nothing. Boolean positional parameters also make
+calls hard to read (`placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)`). Remove `log` and decide
+once whether placements get logged (finding 8 suggests not). The returned `Part` is unused.
+That's fine to keep if you plan to use it.
+
+### 13. Names
+Short names like `plr`, `bt`, `amt`, `p` make the handlers harder to scan. `Main.server.luau`
+already uses `player`. Use `player`, `blockType`, `amount` throughout.
+
+### 14. Remotes are created at runtime
+**Where:** lines 24–34.
+
+This works, but clients have to `WaitForChild` each remote, and nothing shared says what
+arguments each one takes. As the shop grows, consider defining the remotes in one place (a
+`Remotes` folder in the Rojo project, or a shared module that lists names and argument types) so
+the client and server agree. Nothing needs to change right now.
+
+---
+
+## Suggested order of work
+
+1. Validate remote arguments (1) and fix the nil-stock / charged-with-nothing path (4). These
+   are small and close the easiest exploits.
+2. Move coins (and stock) to an in-memory session record saved with `UpdateAsync` (2, 5).
+3. Fix the abandoned-block refund (3) and receipts toggle (6).
+4. Decide on anchoring (7) and add rate limiting (8).
+5. Type and cleanup work (9–14) while you're in the file.
